# Approved production models. build_package.sh --model <path> REFUSES anything
# not listed here with a matching SHA-256 for BOTH the engine and its sidecar.
#
# This is why model selection is not a models/*.engine glob: a glob is accidental
# directory-content selection, and models/ is git-ignored, so a forgotten
# experimental engine with a valid sidecar would silently reach production.
#
# The PAIR is approved, not just the engine: TrtEngine's ctor reads
# <stem>.names.json for class names, so a wrong/modified sidecar changes
# application semantics just as much as a wrong engine, and would otherwise
# ship unchecked as long as the engine hash matched.
#
# Format: <stem> <sha256-of-.engine> <sha256-of-.names.json> <approval reference>
# Only columns 1-3 are parsed; column 4 is the human approval reference.
#
# ENGINE-ONLY ARTIFACT POLICY. The production artifact pair is
# <model-id>.engine + <model-id>.names.json, and the approved ENGINE BYTES are
# the provenance authority. No .onnx or .pt is required, packaged, searched for
# or relied upon by runtime loading, approval, provenance, manifest generation,
# packaging, deployment or acceptance testing. No row here asserts an ONNX or PT
# lineage, and none may.
#
# To approve an engine: put the exact bytes on the target device, validate them
# DIRECTLY there (regular file; sha256 recorded; TensorRT deserialization; one
# real synthetic inference; input and output bindings and shapes; class count
# against the canonical sidecar; sidecar present and valid JSON; identity and
# family; decoder matches the runtime; validated on the target TRT/CUDA/SM
# platform), record every observation in the stem's descriptor `approval` block,
# then add or update the row. gen_model_manifest.py re-measures both files and
# refuses if they disagree with the descriptor or with that approval record.
#
# A REBUILT ENGINE IS A NEW ARTIFACT, NEVER A HASH CORRECTION. A TensorRT plan
# may differ byte-for-byte after a rebuild; that is expected and is not grounds
# for rejection. It does mean the new bytes need their own explicit approval
# before this row changes. Hash enforcement is never relaxed: the runtime keeps
# refusing any .engine whose bytes do not match the approved SHA-256.
#
# ORDERING RULE (spec 8.7.2): a `float-*` stem may only be approved once the
# Slice-7 warm-up allow-list exists, because EngineRegistry::warm_up() scans the
# whole models directory and would otherwise deserialize and run a Ball Leveler
# engine on a Digital Number Reader appliance at every boot. That rule is
# MACHINE-ENFORCED by assert_float_seeding_guarded (packaging/lib/gen_payload.sh),
# which refuses the build if either Float line below is present without a real
# definition of loadable_model_files in src/core/models/compatibility.cpp AND a
# real call to it from src/app/ui/startup.cpp. Both exist as of Slice 7.
digitv3 5247c3f3a70ce1b09b8807b7ed4bb27f7badd972b8628f355baac3ec434cf45a 47538d1023947674faffb92a905429bc9deaa0890f0549d4c1bed1d1a33f9026 directly validated + approved on Jetson .15 (TRT 10.3/CUDA 12.6/SM 87), out [1,300,6] decode_yolo_end2end, 10 classes; see packaging/manifest/digitv3.descriptor.json approval
float-small 129fe461e55f7eb2aaac894c3021016ccd20bfe6be8422215bddbb6157ef4f27 cb7554643e37d6105b0ce54861f665b4b7a954fce8f3c2dcbb15d09262dcabe4 directly validated + approved on Jetson .15 2026-07-30 (TRT 10.3/CUDA 12.6/SM 87), out [1,5,8400] decode_yolo, 1 class; rebuilt engine, new bytes explicitly approved; see packaging/manifest/float-small.descriptor.json approval
float-big a95ffadca3aa96622d40d338505ccd7cfdf57b1faec62efa0645a32739e89351 a675f5e0145a1aa9735b4b59c18b8e30e829e62cf15a62d4f39656ebe238de66 directly validated + approved on Jetson .15 2026-07-30 (TRT 10.3/CUDA 12.6/SM 87), out [1,5,8400] decode_yolo, 1 class; rebuilt engine, new bytes explicitly approved; see packaging/manifest/float-big.descriptor.json approval
